  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B013",
        "laureates": [
          "Mehran Kardar",
          "Yi-Cheng Zhang",
          "Kazumasa A. Takeuchi"
        ],
        "citation": "for revealing and experimentally confirming universal fluctuation laws in non-equilibrium growth"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B003",
    "B050"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration is unchanged. KPZ is an unusually powerful instance of physics extracting a universal and quantitatively testable mechanism from complex, far-from-equilibrium systems. The defining framework of Kardar and Zhang generated predictions far beyond one model system, while Takeuchi's precision experiments tested detailed fluctuation behavior rather than merely qualitative scaling. This convergence of theory, exact results, simulation, and experiment establishes both maturity and a physically meaningful boundary: recognition of universal non-equilibrium growth laws, not of every later application or development.",
    "case_against": "A universality class necessarily has a broad intellectual history, so the three names must not be read as credit for all mathematical advances, numerical work, or experimental realizations. The central remaining issue is whether Takeuchi's experimental confirmation is sufficiently indispensable to the prize-defining achievement relative to other contributors to the theory and its verification.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson argues that optical lattice clocks offer a sharply bounded magic-wavelength principle, exceptional reproducibility, and a compact Katori-Ye attribution.",
        "response": "I agree that B003 is the clearest competing configuration and that its reproducibility is a major merit, not merely an engineering accomplishment. Yet the historical question of where lattice-clock invention ends and the wider optical-clock enterprise begins remains material. KPZ has a comparably definite physical core: a simple equation whose nontrivial universal consequences were subsequently subjected to unusually discriminating experimental tests. I retain B013 because this mechanism changed how non-equilibrium fluctuations are understood across disparate systems."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll favors B003 because its experimental achievement is already circumscribed, whereas the balance between original KPZ theory and later experimental confirmation needs a more exact award boundary.",
        "response": "The warning is well taken. I therefore keep the KPZ citation narrowly tied to universal fluctuation laws in non-equilibrium growth and to their experimental confirmation, rather than to the entire subsequent field. Takeuchi's role is justified only because his measurements tested distinctive quantitative predictions of the framework. This theory-experiment chain is not less mature for being cross-system; it is the reason the result rises above a useful phenomenological model."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "lindroth-eva",
        "point": "Lindroth regards the recollision model and quantum theory of high-harmonic generation as a coherent, mature account of an unexpected strong-field mechanism, subject to separation from the attosecond advances recognized in 2023.",
        "response": "I continue to find B050 an elegant alternative with a strong explanatory narrative. The stated prior-recognition boundary is nevertheless consequential and cannot be settled by broadening the citation. B013 has the advantage that its central achievement is distinct: experimentally verified universal statistics in non-equilibrium growth, rather than a component of an already recognized experimental programme."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether the proposed allocation among foundational formulation and decisive experimental confirmation is historically complete within the three-person limit.",
      "How narrowly the citation must be interpreted to preserve the distinction between KPZ universality and its many later theoretical and experimental developments."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
